Reviewer objective: address the architecture, security and integration review without renaming the com.clgo.dash / cl-go-dash compatibility identity.

Decisions: vault startup failures cross IPC only as a generic signal while filtered detail stays in rotating structured logs; Rust provider DTOs remain the source of truth for generated TypeScript contracts; desktop E2E uses an explicit e2e feature, embedded WebDriver, an isolated temporary profile, an ephemeral vault, and disabled host mutations; Ollama resolves the active managed port at request time so clients created before sidecar startup cannot retain 11434; MCP and Ollama integration tests exercise real process and HTTP boundaries.

Post-review corrections: CI now runs clippy and Rust tests with the e2e feature; release application builds reject that control feature at compile time while the helper remains buildable; logs redact before final truncation, include UTC timestamps, and use meaningful info/error levels; E2E cleanup is idempotent and cannot mask the primary failure; forecast capabilities and experimental model specifications use named fields instead of copied positional booleans or inaccurate clippy justifications.

Validation: cargo test --all: 3035 passed, 2 ignored; cargo test --all --features e2e: 3038 passed, 2 ignored; strict clippy passed with and without e2e; npm test: 411 files / 1875 frontend tests plus 40 build, 6 CEF, 6 E2E-runner, and 51 extension-host tests passed; real native onboarding E2E: 1 passed; TypeScript, ESLint, Rust formatting, Rust-to-TypeScript contract check, release-feature guard, Graphify update, git diff check, and both npm audits passed with zero vulnerabilities.

Review focus: dynamic Ollama port selection, startup IPC and log redaction, the feature-gated E2E isolation boundary, generated provider DTO mappings, MCP direct spawn validation, and removal of misleading too_many_arguments exceptions. Brand identifier rename remains deliberately out of scope.
